<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elias I, Count of Maine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1110">1110</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Elias of La Flèche</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elias of La Flèche (died 1110) was Count of Maine from the 1090s. He resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, who captured him in 1098, and after his release recovered Maine in 1100. His daughter and heiress Ermengarde married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulk-king-of-jerusalem">Fulk V of Anjou</a>, uniting Maine with Anjou.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Elias, also written Hélie or Élie and called de la Flèche or de Baugency, was the son of Jean de la Flèche and Paula, a daughter of Count Herbert I of Maine. He succeeded his father as the third lord of La Flèche. In 1090 he married Matilda, daughter of Gervais II, Lord of Château-du-Loir. In the revolt of 1091 that made his cousin Hugh V Count of Maine, Elias took the castle of Ballon and imprisoned Hoel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>, at La Flèche. Unable to hold the county, Hugh sold it to Elias for 10,000 shillings in 1092.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elias took the cross in 1096 but stayed at home when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a> made clear that he meant to retake Maine. In the fighting of 1098, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-of-belleme">Robert of Bellême</a> was William's main ally, Elias was captured on 28 April. Count Fulk IV of Anjou, whose son Geoffrey was engaged to Elias's daughter, then entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>, but a failed Angevin attack on Ballon let William force a treaty that freed Elias and left Maine in Norman hands. After Matilda's death in March 1099 Elias besieged Le Mans again; the city burned, but the Norman garrison held out and William drove him back.</p><p>When William died in 1100, Elias and Fulk took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a> and expelled the garrison. King Henry Beauclerc probably gave up his claim to Maine in return for Elias's support, and from then on Elias backed him in nearly every regional conflict. He led a contingent from Maine in 1105, was at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a>, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tinchebray">Battle of Tinchebray</a> in 1106 commanded the Manceaux and Breton infantry, which inflicted heavy losses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orderic-vitalis">Orderic Vitalis</a>, Elias married in 1109 Agnes, daughter of Duke William VIII of Aquitaine and widow of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a>, but Orderic probably confused two of Alfonso's wives, and the bride was likely his French widow Beatrice. Elias died on 11 July 1110 and was buried in the choir of the abbey church of Notre-Dame de la Couture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>. Maine passed to his daughter and her husband, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulk-king-of-jerusalem">Fulk V of Anjou</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elias I of Maine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>William the Conqueror took the county in 1063, but Count <b>Elias of La Flèche</b> recovered it, and his daughter's marriage to Fulk V joined it to Anjou.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-maine">County of Maine</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47,2&amp;year=1100" aria-label="France on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4597 513 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Elias I, Count of Maine"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5047 783h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5047 783h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47,2&amp;year=1100">What was here in 1100? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
